Another option is to introduce the average which gives:
$$a+b = 2 \cdot 99 = 198$$
I notice that this would always have to be even as $a > b$:
$$99 + b = 2 a \Rightarrow 99 > a > \sqrt{b} > 0$$
$$31.4 > a > \sqrt{b} $$
$$31.3 > a > \sqrt{b} \ge 0$$
The possible options are $a = 31, a = 32, a = 33, a = 34$ (by $198/2$, both of 99 and 100 work, but 100 can't end in 4 as it would simply mean that $a \times b$ would result in something more than 200).
We thus end up normally under the option space if we use this approach: By the same argument, we'll get a corresponding $b$. Given that, we must equate the total possible squares using our knowledge as a mean which makes all of your cases valid.

